solution:
Since $\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = 0,$ $\mathbf{a}$ is orthogonal to $\mathbf{b},$ so $\mathbf{a}$ lies in the plane determined by $\mathbf{b}$ and $\mathbf{c}.$

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, C;

A = dir(120);
B = dir(210);
C = dir(330);

draw((-1.5,0)--(1.5,0));
draw((0,-1.5)--(0,1.5));
draw(A--B,Arrow(6));
draw(A--C,Arrow(6));

label("$\mathbf{b}$", B, SW);
label("$\mathbf{c}$", C, SE);
label("$\mathbf{a}$", A, N);
[/asy]

Since $\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{c} = 0,$ $\mathbf{a}$ is orthogonal to $\mathbf{c},$ so $\mathbf{a}$ is orthogonal to $\mathbf{b}$ and $\mathbf{c}.$  Therefore, $\mathbf{a}$ is orthogonal to $\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c},$ so $\mathbf{a}$ is parallel to $\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}$.

Therefore, $\mathbf{a} = k (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c})$ for some constant $k.$

Since $\mathbf{a}$ is a unit vector,
\[1 = |\mathbf{a}| = |k (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c})| = |k| |\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}| = |k| |\mathbf{b}| |\mathbf{c}| \sin \frac{\pi}{4} = |k| \cdot 1 \cdot 1 \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{|k|}{\sqrt{2}}.\]Hence, $|k| = \sqrt{2}.$

Therefore, $k$ can be $\sqrt{2}$ or $-\sqrt{2},$ so the possible values of $k$ are $\boxed{\sqrt{2}, -\sqrt{2}}.$The answer is \sqrt{2},-\sqrt{2}

To compute $3 \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -8 \end{pmatrix} - 2 \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix}$,
we can distribute the scalar multiplication and then subtract the two vectors component-wise:
\begin{align*}
3 \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -8 \end{pmatrix} - 2 \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \cdot 2 \\ 3 \cdot -8 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 2 \cdot 1 \\ 2 \cdot -7 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -24 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -14 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 6 - 2 \\ -24 - (-14) \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -10 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
So, $3 \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -8 \end{pmatrix} - 2 \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix} = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -10 \end{pmatrix}}$.The answer is \begin{pmatrix}4\-10\end{pmatrix}

