Note: The normal at a point $P$ on a curve $\mathcal{C}$ is the line passing through $P$ that is perpendicular to the tangent to $\mathcal{C}$ at $P.$

solution:
We start by finding $f(g(x))$ and $g(f(x))$.
\begin{align*}
f(g(x)) &= f(Bx + A) \\
&= A(Bx + A) + B \\
&= ABx + A^2 + B
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
g(f(x)) &= g(Ax + B) \\
&= B(Ax + B) + A \\
&= ABx + B^2 + A
\end{align*}
Now we substitute these expressions into the equation $f(g(x)) - g(f(x)) = B - A$.
\begin{align*}
ABx + A^2 + B - (ABx + B^2 + A) &= B - A \\
A^2 + B - B^2 - A &= B - A \\
A^2 - B^2 &= 0 \\
(A - B)(A + B) &= 0
\end{align*}
Since $A \neq B$, we must have $A + B = \boxed{0}$.The answer is 0

